    "body": "**The three open questions, answered from the sources** (behaviour only, per the Clean-Room Protocol).\n\nSources: Tormach TD10749 *Owner's Guide: microARC 4 - 100 mm 4th Axis* (1223A); TD10552 *Owner's Guide: 6 in. and 8 in. 4th Axis for M/MX* (0626A); TD10374 *4th Axis, PCNC 1100 / PCNC 770* (0626A); the public LinuxCNC G-code reference (linuxcnc.org/docs, \"Machining Center\" overview §2.2 rotary axes and §2.5 feed rate, and the G93/G94/G95 section); Tormach's PathPilot post-processor guidelines (they list G93, G94 and G95 as supported).\n\n**1. Does PathPilot wrap A, or treat it as linear degrees?** The sources don't settle it.\n- None of the three Tormach guides says. TD10749 says only that PathPilot v2.7.2 or later is needed and the axis type is set under Settings > 4th Axis Rotary > \"4\" 4th Axis\". You jog A with the keys or the shuttle, and the chuck-centring step jogs it through a full 360 degrees.\n- LinuxCNC gives rotary positions in degrees, unbounded either way by default. Rollover is a per-machine configuration option (a wrapped rotary joint), and PathPilot's own configuration isn't public.\n- **Taken (conservative):** this PR does indexing only. Each index posts an absolute `A` inside the travel the machine file states (-360..360 on the new 770MX + microARC 4 file). An index lands on the same angle under either reading; a wrapped axis might only take the shorter way round, and it turns with the tool retracted to machine Z0. Any A that changes inside an operation (wrapped milling, the rotary kinds) is refused by name until PathPilot's rollover is confirmed on the hardware.\n\n**2. Is there an A clamp or brake M-code on the microARC 4?** No.\n- TD10749 describes a sealed harmonic-drive unit with no clamp, no brake and no M-code.\n- The older tables clamp by hand. TD10552 and TD10374 describe manual rotary-clamp handles, plus tilt clamps on the tilting tables. The clamps are to be \"loosened during motorized rotation\", and TD10374's troubleshooting lists \"table lock(s) engaged\" as a reason the 4th axis won't move. No document gives an M-code.\n- **Taken:** the post writes no clamp code. At the first index it writes an operator note to release any manual A clamp before indexing, and that WCS Y0 Z0 must sit on the A centerline.\n\n**3. Feed for combined XYZA moves: G94 or G93?**\n- LinuxCNC G94: when any of X, Y and Z move, F is the XYZ feed in length per minute and the rotary is carried along so all axes start and stop together. Only a move of the rotary alone reads F as degrees per minute. G93 (inverse time) is in LinuxCNC and in Tormach's guideline list.\n- Neither has been run on a microARC 4 yet.\n- **Taken:** this PR posts no combined move at all. A moves only alone, as `G0 A..`, after `G53 G0 Z<safe>`, between operations. The header stays G94, and no A word ever rides a cutting move.\n- Simultaneous 4-axis (wrapped A milling, probably under G93) is split off to a follow-up issue, to settle on the Hub and the hardware.\n\nRecorded in `docs/decisions/785-microarc-4th-axis.md` on the branch.\n",
    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
